I did look a bit closer at amd and indeed it has a habit of doing things
internally without paying a closer attention to what was asked.  More
precisely, a version 6.0.3 which you will find included in RH 6.2
distribution, boasts in its documentation that it supports NFSv3 but it
seems that to make it to actually _notice_ that this is available would
be not that simple.  I did try, but not very hard, because a later version
6.0.5 from the current 'rawhide' already has an extra patch for NFSv3 on
Linux and readily recompiles for glibc-2.1 as well after minor tweaks
(and, undoubtely, equally easy for other distributions with slightly
different startup files and/or shared libraries).  It first tries NFSv3
mount and if that fails it drops down a version and tries again so you do
not have to have all automounted NFS servers at the same version level.
You will see with it in your log files something of that sort:

 amd[21631]: recompute_portmap: NFS version 3
 amd[21631]: Using MOUNT version: 3
 amd[21631]: Using NFS version 3, protocol udp on ....
 amd[10515]: amfs_host_fmount: NFS version 3
......
 amd[21631]: get_nfs_version trying a lower version
....
 amd[21631]: Using NFS version 2, protocol tcp on <other host>
 amd[21639]: amfs_host_fmount: NFS version 2
....

The above assumes, of course, that you do have a kernel with NFSv3
support turned on and other infrastucture (like proper startup scripts).

If you cannot be bothered with a compiler then you can find all
pieces, including assorted Alpha binaries, via anonymous ftp from
ftp://ftp.harddata.com/pub/NFS-v3-Alpha/
but truly there is not much to it and all of this, with an exception
of few binaries :-), is not even tied to Alpha in some particular way.

At the URL above there are no binaries for other platforms.  Ladies
and gentelmen, please find out what 'rpm --rebuild ...' does. :-)

  Michal (mj)



