In comp.os.linux.alpha Michal Szymanski <msz@tucana.astrouw.edu.pl> wrote:

> The 2.2.19 kernel recently announced by RedHat and available for all
> platforms supported brings long awaited NFS version 3 (at least to 2.2.x
> series, 2.4.x probably have had it for some time now).

That is correct if you were waiting for an "official" release.  In
form of kernel patches and self-updates to tools NFS version 3 existed
on Alpha for a very long time (although you could be hit by bugs
from on occasions :-).  Don't remember now for how long but probably
something around a year.  This, of course, required also 'mount'
which will support NFS3, and which Red Hat supplied in the latest
updates, but nothing was stopping you from recompiling it from
a newer version of util-linux by yourself.

> but ... *only* when I use "mount" command. Normally I have the AMD
> automounter running to automatically mount/umount file systems from
> all machines over the LAN. Unfortunately, "amd" apparently cannot
> mount NFSv3.

Hm, lets see ("thebox" is a box on my "home" network):

$ ls /net/thebox/
....
$ mount
....
thebox:/ on /.automount/thebox/root type nfs
(soft,nosuid,nodev,nfsvers=3,rsize=8192,wsize=8192,dev=00000007)
....

It at least _claims_ to be a version 3. :-)

You have a file /etc/amd.net where you can set various default options.
You can also have options specific for particular hosts.  Granted,
amd documentation (rpm -qd am-utils), and 'info am-utils' in particular,
is not the clearest thing I ever seen but descriptions are there.
Manpage for mount clearly states that a default NFS version used
is 2 so if you desire something else you have to be explicit.

It may be also possible to do that with 'autofs' instead of 'amd'
but I did not look at this closer yet.

  Michal


