I am a big Debian fan, but really, support for the older Alpha systems in Linux
is, well, disappointing. Of course, that's my opinion. I should know, I have 3
2100's running here and Linux was a major pain. I tried RH 7.0, Suse, and Debian
2.2 (which ran the best). X Windows did not work on any system I had, it loaded
generic support for SCSI, so that means you have to install on the built-in EISA
controlled drives (If you have a pedastal box) then move your filesystems around
if you so desired. If you are using a SWXCR card, forget about installing
anything except Windows on that card. :p DAC960 support doesn't come out of the
box. The EISA card I mentioned? It's a Sym810 or NCR810, again, nothing
supporting it during installation.

Which brings me to my issue. My friend came over and installed FreeBSD 4.2 on
one of my 2100's here. It is absolutely fabulous! Everything works correctly,
there is no issue as far as kernel compiles/re-compiles. (I never get anything
past 2.218pre running on these machines when Linux was installed) I am going to
be converting the rest of these machines over to FreeBSD as time permit.

Just something to think about. Am I bashing Linux? No. Am I saying that Linux
could use a little help when it comes to older Alpha support? Yeah.

Just my $0.02 worth.

AJ Schroeder

