> mind.
> 
> I guess at a minimum I'd like something which allows the specification
> of the relationships between a small number of tables  ( but I've no
> idea how that's done ! ), and some simple GUI front end for adding
> test data, submitting queries and specifying report formats.  Or will
> I have to write my own C++ applications to do all that ?
> 
> So, what's available for Alpha ?  What has the nicest design tools ?
> What are the pros and cons I should look out for ?

> What's MySQL ?  Is it a database or just a query language
> implementation ?

MySQL combines: 
 - an ISAM B-tree-based database system, and
 - an SQL query language on top of that.

It only implements part of the ANSI SQL standards, although the
deficiencies are a bit less dramatic than they used to be.  It's
probably fairly adequate for relatively simple web-based apps.

> Are there any databases bundled with RH6.2 which I may have already
> installed ?

MySQL and PostgreSQL are both likely to be bundled.  PostgreSQL is
definitely more sophisticated than MySQL, and likely more robust if
you need to scale things up.  Either should be pretty useful to learn
about databases.

> Oracle is an obvious big name, but is it available for Alpha ?  If so,
> what does it cost for personal development use, and what exactly do I
> need their web site seems to offer a bewildering choice of products !

On the Oracle side of things, it's very simple: It's not available on
Linux/Alpha.

You can get development copies for IA-32, freely, but it gets pretty
expensive when deploying for production purposes.
