: anything that uses floating point will beat x86s with hands tied behind
: the alpha's back, esp. when you use the compaq (aka dec) compiler which
: is a free download.  gcc supposedly is relatively weak for alpha, but that
: is supposed to change with gcc3.0

Like all excessive generalizations, this statement is false. Alpha EV56 at 533
Mhz, while still a quite respectable floating-point workhorse, is nowhere close
to having an overwhelming advantage over contemporary PCs. On a variery of 
floating-point intensive codes I have to use daily, it gives somewhere between
50 and 70% of the performance of your mundane PIII-933. The current crop of
Athlons fares even better in comparison. Of course, I have no doubt that there
exist applications where EV56-533 would be a much better choice than a PIII or
Athlon, even today; it is just that the raw CPU performance is not really it.

This is not particularly surprising, either - EV56-533 is at least four years
old. _At_that_time_ it did have an overwhelming advantage over PCs in
performance, and even a significant advantage in price/performance (again, for
our applications. YMMV). However, quite a lot have changed since that time ...

